      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-10871",
        "knowledge_point_id": "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0",
        "knowledge_point": "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line",
        "problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+x$ at the point $(1,3)$ intersects the $x$-axis.",
        "worked_solution": "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+x)=4x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=4(1)+1=5,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{5}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{1}{5}+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{16}{5} & =0 \\\\ x & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(16,0).$",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "type": "radio",
            "choices": [
              {
                "type": "math",
                "value": "(1,0)",
                "option": "0",
