        "knowledge_point": "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient",
        "problem": "Rewrite $2{x}^{2}-4x+3$ by completing the square.",
        "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2({x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{3}{2}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}) & =2((x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2{(x-1)}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2{(x-1)}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
