      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-112524",
        "knowledge_point_id": "257b907c-70c1-5bbd-a6d2-297d51325d1f",
        "knowledge_point": "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Positive Leading Coefficient",
        "problem": "The equation $4{x}^{2}+6x-5=0$ can be written in the form $4(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q.$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6x-5 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x) & =5 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =5 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =5 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}] & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =5 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =5 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{29}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $q=\\frac{29}{4}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{29}{4}=\\frac{32}{4}=8.\n$$",
        "answer_fields": [
